1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,833
Your app runs in a container, on a server, inside a cloud network. So who can reach whom?

2
00:00:06,383 --> 00:00:09,455
The answer is always the same: find the boundaries.

3
00:00:10,005 --> 00:00:13,512
A boundary is a place where the rules change: the address range,

4
00:00:13,512 --> 00:00:14,060
the route,

5
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:15,595
and what is allowed through.

6
00:00:16,145 --> 00:00:19,049
Today you cross four of them: client to server,

7
00:00:19,049 --> 00:00:20,161
host to container,

8
00:00:20,161 --> 00:00:21,520
container to internet,

9
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:22,694
and a cloud subnet.

10
00:00:23,244 --> 00:00:28,023
First on a small network built on this computer, then live in a real cloud account.

11
00:00:30,073 --> 00:00:34,041
Start with one question: where does one network end, and another begin?

12
00:00:34,591 --> 00:00:35,744
Inside one network,

13
00:00:35,744 --> 00:00:37,869
machines reach each other directly.

14
00:00:37,869 --> 00:00:38,597
To leave it,

15
00:00:38,597 --> 00:00:39,751
they need a router,

16
00:00:39,751 --> 00:00:40,479
the gateway.

17
00:00:41,029 --> 00:00:42,066
At every boundary,

18
00:00:42,066 --> 00:00:44,544
three things can change: the address range,

19
00:00:44,544 --> 00:00:45,120
the route,

20
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:47,194
and the rules about what is allowed.

21
00:00:47,744 --> 00:00:49,660
So when something cannot connect,

22
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:50,415
do not guess.

23
00:00:50,415 --> 00:00:52,446
Ask: which boundary is it crossing,

24
00:00:52,446 --> 00:00:53,782
and what changed there?

25
00:00:54,332 --> 00:00:55,348
Here is the lab.

26
00:00:55,348 --> 00:00:55,919
A client,

27
00:00:55,919 --> 00:00:56,491
a router,

28
00:00:56,491 --> 00:00:57,888
and behind the router,

29
00:00:57,888 --> 00:00:59,921
a host that runs two containers,

30
00:00:59,921 --> 00:01:02,652
plus a server standing in for the internet.

31
00:01:04,702 --> 00:01:06,327
You are the client,

32
00:01:06,327 --> 00:01:08,808
ten dot zero dot one dot two.

33
00:01:08,808 --> 00:01:11,032
How do I reach the router?

34
00:01:11,032 --> 00:01:13,342
Type: ip route get 10.0.1.1

35
00:01:13,472 --> 00:01:18,421
Directly, on dev c zero, with no via. The router sits on my own network.

36
00:01:18,971 --> 00:01:24,646
Now the host, on the other side. Type: ip route get 10.0.2.2

37
00:01:24,776 --> 00:01:28,192
This time it says via ten dot zero dot one dot one.

38
00:01:28,192 --> 00:01:30,269
The host is on another network,

39
00:01:30,269 --> 00:01:33,082
so my packets must go through the gateway.

40
00:01:33,082 --> 00:01:34,355
That is a boundary.

41
00:01:34,905 --> 00:01:37,083
And the container,

42
00:01:37,083 --> 00:01:42,651
one seventy-two dot eighteen dot zero dot two?

43
00:01:42,651 --> 00:01:46,403
Type: ping -c 1 -W 1 172.18.0.2

44
00:01:46,533 --> 00:01:48,290
Destination net unreachable,

45
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:49,294
from the router.

46
00:01:49,294 --> 00:01:51,302
It has no route to that network.

47
00:01:51,302 --> 00:01:52,180
From out here,

48
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:53,999
the container does not exist.

49
00:01:56,050 --> 00:01:57,054
Now containers.

50
00:01:57,054 --> 00:01:59,866
A container is not a tiny virtual machine.

51
00:01:59,866 --> 00:02:01,607
It is an ordinary process,

52
00:02:01,607 --> 00:02:04,285
with its own private view of the system.

53
00:02:04,835 --> 00:02:07,097
One part of that view is the network.

54
00:02:07,097 --> 00:02:11,011
The container gets its own network namespace: its own addresses,

55
00:02:11,011 --> 00:02:12,173
and its own routes.

56
00:02:12,723 --> 00:02:13,708
A virtual cable,

57
00:02:13,708 --> 00:02:14,877
called a veth pair,

58
00:02:14,877 --> 00:02:18,139
joins that namespace to a virtual switch on the host,

59
00:02:18,139 --> 00:02:19,123
called a bridge.

60
00:02:19,673 --> 00:02:21,703
Docker names its bridge docker0.

61
00:02:21,703 --> 00:02:24,239
The host acts as the container's router,

62
00:02:24,239 --> 00:02:26,649
and the bridge address is its gateway.

63
00:02:27,199 --> 00:02:28,950
No Docker is installed here.

64
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:30,950
I built the same pieces by hand,

65
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:33,450
so you can see what Docker does for you.

66
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:36,547
In this lab,

67
00:02:36,547 --> 00:02:42,307
the letters C T R mean: run the next command inside the container.

68
00:02:42,307 --> 00:02:44,140
Type: ctr ip -br addr

69
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:45,330
One interface,

70
00:02:45,330 --> 00:02:46,163
E T H zero,

71
00:02:46,163 --> 00:02:50,251
address one seventy-two dot eighteen dot zero dot two.

72
00:02:50,251 --> 00:02:51,613
A private network,

73
00:02:51,613 --> 00:02:52,825
inside the host.

74
00:02:53,375 --> 00:02:56,276
Its routes. Type: ctr ip route

75
00:02:56,406 --> 00:02:58,902
The default route points at the bridge,

76
00:02:58,902 --> 00:03:01,846
one seventy-two dot eighteen dot zero dot one.

77
00:03:01,846 --> 00:03:03,894
That is the container's gateway.

78
00:03:04,444 --> 00:03:12,444
Reach the other container. Type: ctr ping -c 1 172.18.0.3

79
00:03:12,574 --> 00:03:14,175
Same bridge,

80
00:03:14,175 --> 00:03:15,777
so it works.

81
00:03:15,777 --> 00:03:18,713
Now the outside world.

82
00:03:18,713 --> 00:03:24,585
Type: ctr curl -sS -m 2 10.0.3.2:8080/whoami

83
00:03:24,715 --> 00:03:29,728
Timed out. The request left, but nobody outside knows how to answer a private address.

84
00:03:30,278 --> 00:03:32,187
The fix is N A T,

85
00:03:32,187 --> 00:03:33,534
on the host.

86
00:03:33,534 --> 00:03:34,882
In this lab,

87
00:03:34,882 --> 00:03:37,352
H nat means: iptables,

88
00:03:37,352 --> 00:03:39,261
in the nat table,

89
00:03:39,261 --> 00:03:40,609
on the host.

90
00:03:40,609 --> 00:03:46,897
Type: hnat -A POSTROUTING -s 172.18.0.0/24 -j MASQUERADE

91
00:03:47,027 --> 00:03:57,203
Same request again. Type: ctr curl -sS -m 2 10.0.3.2:8080/whoami

92
00:03:57,333 --> 00:03:59,752
You are ten dot zero dot two dot two.

93
00:03:59,752 --> 00:04:01,387
The server sees the host,

94
00:04:01,387 --> 00:04:02,563
not the container.

95
00:04:02,563 --> 00:04:04,917
The container hides behind the host,

96
00:04:04,917 --> 00:04:07,402
like your laptop behind a home router.

97
00:04:09,452 --> 00:04:14,231
One more direction. Someone outside wants to reach a service inside the container.

98
00:04:14,781 --> 00:04:16,871
The container's address is private,

99
00:04:16,871 --> 00:04:18,663
so outsiders cannot aim at it.

100
00:04:18,663 --> 00:04:20,455
They can only aim at the host.

101
00:04:21,005 --> 00:04:22,748
So the host listens on a port,

102
00:04:22,748 --> 00:04:25,187
and forwards the traffic to the container.

103
00:04:25,187 --> 00:04:27,277
Docker calls this publishing a port.

104
00:04:27,827 --> 00:04:28,521
Underneath,

105
00:04:28,521 --> 00:04:30,475
it is a destination N A T rule.

106
00:04:30,475 --> 00:04:32,933
Masquerade rewrites the source address.

107
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:34,825
This rewrites the destination.

108
00:04:36,875 --> 00:04:38,835
Back on the client.

109
00:04:38,835 --> 00:04:43,063
The host is ten dot zero dot two dot two.

110
00:04:43,063 --> 00:04:46,570
Is anything on port eighty eighty?

111
00:04:46,570 --> 00:04:49,974
Type: curl -sS -m 2 10.0.2.2:8080

112
00:04:50,104 --> 00:04:54,285
Failed to connect. The host is there, but nothing is listening on that port.

113
00:04:54,835 --> 00:04:55,955
Now publish it,

114
00:04:55,955 --> 00:04:57,523
the way Docker would.

115
00:04:57,523 --> 00:04:58,419
In this lab,

116
00:04:58,419 --> 00:05:02,078
publish forwards a host port to a container port.

117
00:05:02,078 --> 00:05:03,795
Type: publish 8080 8000

118
00:05:03,925 --> 00:05:10,666
Ask again. Type: curl -s 10.0.2.2:8080/whoami

119
00:05:10,796 --> 00:05:13,306
You are ten dot zero dot one dot two.

120
00:05:13,306 --> 00:05:14,865
The container answered,

121
00:05:14,865 --> 00:05:16,764
and it sees the real client.

122
00:05:16,764 --> 00:05:19,138
Only the destination was rewritten.

123
00:05:19,688 --> 00:05:24,189
Look at the rule behind it. Type: hnat -S PREROUTING

124
00:05:24,319 --> 00:05:24,723
D nat,

125
00:05:24,723 --> 00:05:28,025
to one seventy-two dot eighteen dot zero dot two,

126
00:05:28,025 --> 00:05:29,373
port eight thousand.

127
00:05:29,373 --> 00:05:31,530
That is all a published port is.

128
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:35,879
Now the same ideas in the cloud.

129
00:05:35,879 --> 00:05:37,172
In Amazon's cloud,

130
00:05:37,172 --> 00:05:39,472
your private network is a V P C,

131
00:05:39,472 --> 00:05:41,196
a virtual private cloud.

132
00:05:41,746 --> 00:05:48,701
You choose its address range, like 10.20.0.0/16, and cut it into subnets.

133
00:05:49,251 --> 00:05:54,520
A public subnet has a route to an internet gateway. A private subnet has no such route.

134
00:05:55,070 --> 00:05:57,176
That route is the only difference.

135
00:05:57,176 --> 00:06:00,026
A subnet is public because of its route table,

136
00:06:00,026 --> 00:06:01,513
not because of its name.

137
00:06:02,063 --> 00:06:07,225
A security group is the firewall around each server. By default, it lets nothing in.

138
00:06:07,775 --> 00:06:11,346
A private server that must reach out needs a N A T gateway.

139
00:06:11,346 --> 00:06:12,616
That one costs money,

140
00:06:12,616 --> 00:06:14,069
so we will not build it.

141
00:06:14,619 --> 00:06:15,794
Now watch it live,

142
00:06:15,794 --> 00:06:16,969
in a real account.

143
00:06:16,969 --> 00:06:19,123
These are real screen recordings.

144
00:06:19,123 --> 00:06:21,147
My account details are blurred.

145
00:06:23,197 --> 00:06:25,867
The console builds the network: a V P C,

146
00:06:25,867 --> 00:06:26,668
two subnets,

147
00:06:26,668 --> 00:06:27,803
two route tables,

148
00:06:27,803 --> 00:06:29,405
and an internet gateway.

149
00:06:29,955 --> 00:06:32,274
Here is the public route table.

150
00:06:32,274 --> 00:06:35,341
Ten dot twenty slash sixteen stays local.

151
00:06:35,341 --> 00:06:41,027
And zero dot zero dot zero dot zero slash zero goes to the internet gateway.

152
00:06:41,577 --> 00:06:46,761
That second line is the whole definition of public: everything else leaves through the gateway.

153
00:06:47,311 --> 00:06:48,911
Now the private route table.

154
00:06:49,461 --> 00:06:51,024
One route only: local.

155
00:06:51,024 --> 00:06:53,155
Nothing leads to the internet.

156
00:06:53,155 --> 00:06:55,997
A server here can talk inside the V P C,

157
00:06:55,997 --> 00:06:57,205
and nowhere else.

158
00:06:57,755 --> 00:07:02,939
The resource map draws it for you. One subnet routes to the internet. The other does not.

159
00:07:04,989 --> 00:07:06,431
Now two real servers,

160
00:07:06,431 --> 00:07:07,736
one in each subnet.

161
00:07:07,736 --> 00:07:09,315
Both are T three micro,

162
00:07:09,315 --> 00:07:12,200
marked free tier eligible in this account.

163
00:07:12,750 --> 00:07:15,715
Look at the security group: no inbound rules at all.

164
00:07:15,715 --> 00:07:18,851
Nothing from the internet can connect to either server.

165
00:07:19,401 --> 00:07:20,660
Here they are, running.

166
00:07:21,210 --> 00:07:23,615
The public server has a public address.

167
00:07:23,615 --> 00:07:25,342
The private server has none.

168
00:07:25,342 --> 00:07:26,143
Same account,

169
00:07:26,143 --> 00:07:26,945
same network,

170
00:07:26,945 --> 00:07:27,994
different subnet.

171
00:07:28,544 --> 00:07:35,690
The private server's own page shows only a private address: ten dot twenty dot one thirty-three dot one fourteen.

172
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:37,982
Now the most important step.

173
00:07:37,982 --> 00:07:39,724
When you finish a cloud lab,

174
00:07:39,724 --> 00:07:41,092
delete what you built.

175
00:07:41,092 --> 00:07:41,466
First,

176
00:07:41,466 --> 00:07:42,896
terminate both servers.

177
00:07:43,446 --> 00:07:47,841
Then delete the V P C. The console lists everything that goes with it.

178
00:07:48,391 --> 00:07:48,701
Gone.

179
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:50,744
Only the default network is left.

180
00:07:50,744 --> 00:07:51,921
Do this every time,

181
00:07:51,921 --> 00:07:53,469
so nothing keeps running,

182
00:07:53,469 --> 00:07:54,831
and nothing is billed.

183
00:07:54,831 --> 00:07:57,308
Check your own free tier before you try.

184
00:07:59,658 --> 00:08:01,429
Match the symptom to the boundary.

185
00:08:01,979 --> 00:08:06,366
A container cannot be reached from another machine: the port is not published,

186
00:08:06,366 --> 00:08:07,547
or there is no route.

187
00:08:08,097 --> 00:08:12,513
A container cannot reach the internet: no N A T, or no default route.

188
00:08:13,063 --> 00:08:17,393
Refused on the published port: nothing is listening inside the container.

189
00:08:17,943 --> 00:08:22,760
A cloud server is unreachable from the internet: no internet gateway route,

190
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:24,109
or no public address.

191
00:08:24,659 --> 00:08:30,333
A private server cannot download updates: it needs a N A T gateway, or a different design.

192
00:08:30,883 --> 00:08:35,235
Times out only in the cloud: check the security group, then the route table.

193
00:08:37,285 --> 00:08:38,544
Four myths to drop.

194
00:08:39,094 --> 00:08:41,651
Myth one: a container is its own computer.

195
00:08:41,651 --> 00:08:43,356
It shares the host's kernel.

196
00:08:43,356 --> 00:08:44,939
Only its view is separate.

197
00:08:45,489 --> 00:08:48,638
Myth two: a private subnet is secure by itself.

198
00:08:48,638 --> 00:08:52,657
It is only unreachable until someone adds a route or a rule.

199
00:08:53,207 --> 00:08:56,481
Myth three: every server in a public subnet is public.

200
00:08:56,481 --> 00:08:58,360
It also needs a public address,

201
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:00,482
and a rule that allows the traffic.

202
00:09:01,032 --> 00:09:04,361
Myth four: a container's address works outside the host.

203
00:09:04,361 --> 00:09:05,907
It is private to the host,

204
00:09:05,907 --> 00:09:07,453
unless you publish a port.

205
00:09:08,003 --> 00:09:11,480
Find the boundary, then check the address, the route, and the rule.

206
00:09:13,530 --> 00:09:15,855
Here is how you check your own understanding.

207
00:09:16,405 --> 00:09:19,968
Explain what a network boundary is, and what can change at one.

208
00:09:20,518 --> 00:09:22,589
Read the output of ip route get,

209
00:09:22,589 --> 00:09:26,406
and say whether the destination is direct or via a gateway.

210
00:09:26,956 --> 00:09:29,516
Explain how a container reaches the internet.

211
00:09:30,066 --> 00:09:32,114
Explain what publishing a port does.

212
00:09:32,664 --> 00:09:35,779
Explain the difference between a public and a private subnet.

213
00:09:36,329 --> 00:09:39,017
Delete your cloud resources, and explain why.

214
00:09:39,567 --> 00:09:41,188
To pass, you need all six.

215
00:09:43,238 --> 00:09:45,521
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

216
00:09:46,071 --> 00:09:51,084
One: run ip route get 8.8.8.8, and find your gateway.

217
00:09:51,634 --> 00:09:56,114
Two: if Docker is installed, run docker network ls, and find the bridge.

218
00:09:56,664 --> 00:09:57,770
Three: on paper,

219
00:09:57,770 --> 00:10:00,257
draw a V P C with one public subnet,

220
00:10:00,257 --> 00:10:01,570
one private subnet,

221
00:10:01,570 --> 00:10:03,021
and two route tables.

222
00:10:03,571 --> 00:10:06,157
Four: if you build it in a free account,

223
00:10:06,157 --> 00:10:07,709
use free resources only,

224
00:10:07,709 --> 00:10:10,166
and delete everything when you finish.

225
00:10:10,166 --> 00:10:11,071
Servers first,

226
00:10:11,071 --> 00:10:12,041
then the V P C.

227
00:10:12,591 --> 00:10:16,473
Five: for each symptom in the table, write the boundary and the cause.

228
00:10:17,023 --> 00:10:20,330
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

229
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:36,755
Quick check.

230
00:10:37,305 --> 00:10:38,862
What can change at a boundary?

231
00:10:39,412 --> 00:10:41,460
How does a container reach the internet?

232
00:10:42,010 --> 00:10:43,503
What makes a subnet public?

233
00:10:44,053 --> 00:10:45,973
What must you do when a cloud lab ends?

234
00:10:46,523 --> 00:10:50,790
Objective covered: MC03-O07.

235
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:53,409
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

236
00:11:03,959 --> 00:11:09,826
Next: diagnosing D N S, port, firewall, T L S and routing failures in a lab.

237
00:11:10,376 --> 00:11:13,704
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
